# Which operation induces into which layer, reduced to what a `categoryValueRef` carries. One row
# per (filing, operation, layer): this operation induces into this layer, and the flow node says so
# itself rather than being collected by a container.
#
# The direction is the whole difference
#   A `lane` collects its members, and a flow node can be collected once. A `categoryValue` collects
#   nobody: the member declares which categories it is in, without limit, so the categories may
#   overlap and no container has to be enlarged to say so. That is why this relation can be many to
#   many where diagrams/lane_membership.sqlc cannot.
#
# Exercised, not hypothetical
#   On the corpus an operation both draws from `labour` and induces into `capability`, so it belongs
#   to two layers, and a lane set can hold it in one. With no relation for the inductions, an
#   emitter puts the operation in the draw lane and the induction is missing from the artifact
#   entirely.
#
# Which layer, never how much
#   The wider relation carries three values, a unit, a typed blank and `decider`; none of it is
#   composed here, for the reason diagrams/lane_members.sqlc gives.
-- pm:Operation/pm:Induction as which layer, never how much; BPMN 2.0
-- tFlowElement/categoryValueRef.
SELECT i.filing, i.operation, i.layer
FROM (
    :compose(entries/inductions.sqlc)
) i
